content.text:
Now DOM cache in updateHUD. Let's implement helper functions and use for all changes? At least text. Add module-level:
```
const hud = {};
function txt(id, value){ if(hud[id]!==value){hud[id]=value; $(id).textContent=value;} }
function cls(id,name,on){ const key=id+name; if(hud[key]!==on){...}}
function bar(id,pct){ const v=...+ '%'; if(hud[id]!==v)...}
```
This may help. But biggest costs are scene canvas/tach. Let's first test performance after dpr/backdrop/tach cache/pre-roll not yet. Need implement pre-roll and simulation substeps. Then measure.

Pre-roll in startStage:
```
function preRoll(seconds=1.8) {
  engine.starter = true;
  let caught=false;
  const n=Math.round(seconds*60);
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++){
    const rpm=rpmOf(engine);
    engine.starter = !caught && rpm<820;
    engine.step(1/60);
    if(!caught && rpmOf(engine)>800) caught=true;
  }
  engine.starter=false;
  game.hasCaught=caught; game.started=caught;
  game.autoCrank=0;
  if (!caught) game.autoCrank=3.2;
}
```
But engine.step may set starter based on our value each step; okay. Use 1.8s.
Call after setting controls and reset. Then if caught hide coach? Set coach stage tip. The stage starts running; hide coach maybe after 9s. Show stage.coach.
- game.time remains 0.
- If caught, progress conditions can apply next frame. Good.
- Need set `game.autoCrank = caught ? 0 : 3.2`.
- `game.stalledFor=0`.
- `phase='running'`.
- In updateGame, `if (!game.hasCaught) auto` etc.
- At start, engine.totalCycles ~14, okay.

One concern: stage 5 pre-roll 1.8s at load40 throttle50 wall 30; fine.

Simulation catch-up in frame:
```
if phase running and !paused:
  let remaining=Math.min(dt,0.3);
  while (remaining > 1e-4 && phase==='running') { const h=Math.min(remaining,1/60); updateGame(h); remaining-=h; }
```
But `updateGame` includes DOM updates, called multiple times => more DOM; could be costly. Better split physics step from UI. But on normal 60fps, one iteration. At low FPS, several. Could leave cap 0.05 (one) for UI; pre-roll solves screenshot. For actual slow devices, catching up matters. But DOM updates in loop bad. Refactor `updateGame(dt)` into `stepPhysics(dt)` + updates at end? Conditions/hold and UI per step. We can make `updateGame` run internal physics substeps but do UI once:
```
function updateGame(dt){
  const steps = Math.ceil(dt/ (1/60));
  const h=dt/steps;
  for (let i=0;i<steps;i++) tick(h);
  // UI once (need functions)
}
```
Current updateGame mixes. Refactor is more work. Keep frame max dt 0.1 and call updateGame with dt as is; engine.step clamps to 0.05 -> mismatch at low fps. Pre-roll enough. Real users 60fps.

Maybe adjust `engine.step` itself to handle dt by internal while; it already caps remaining to 0.05. Could remove the 0.05 cap? It handles 900 guard with hMax ~0.0012 -> dt 0.1 needs ~83 substeps; guard 900 covers 1s. If dt 1s, 833 substeps per frame heavy but at 1fps could work. Change `let remaining = Math.min(dt, 0.05);` to `Math.min(dt, 0.25)` maybe. This is a physics model; allowing larger frame catch-up better. At low fps, 0.25s simulation. Then frame dt cap 0.25. Good. But game time increments by dt, 0.25. Let's change engine cap to 0.2 and frame dt cap 0.2. Real users unaffected. Not critical.

Pre-roll more important.

Add `window.__sc` already.

Implement pre-roll.

