Maybe better mobile garage layout: single scroll container (already), stage-list max-height none on mobile (all 6 stages), shop items descriptions one line-ish, footer sticky. Then panel content is much taller (6*64=384 + stage header + shop 4*80=320 + header/footer = ~950) and user scrolls, but no nested scroll. Sticky footer shows launch. That might be a better UX. The current nested scroll is a problem (scrolling inside stage list vs panel). At 390x844, panel content 869 only—not huge; making stage list full height adds 148px, total ~1017; scrollable. Sticky footer then always visible. That's fine.

But the instructions in summary said "styled garage scrollbar" and "mobile layout ... stage card at bottom ... control deck". Garage wasn't explicitly polished. It looks acceptable but can be better. Let's implement:
- In @media (max-width: 980px): `.stage-list { max-height: none; overflow: visible; padding-right: 0; }` so the page panel scrolls, not nested. But 6 stage items × ~64 = 384, plus shop 4×~86 = 344, plus heads = ~800, plus header ~120 + footer ~100 = >1000. Scroll.
- `.garage-panel { max-height: 94dvh; overflow-y: auto; padding-bottom: 0? }`.
- `.garage-foot { position: sticky; bottom: 0; background: linear-gradient(180deg, rgba(7,11,14,0.75), rgba(7,11,14,0.995) 30%); padding: 12px 0 calc(8px + env(safe-area-inset-bottom)); margin-top: 14px; }`
- Maybe give it a top border.
- And remove nested scrollbar.

However, sticky inside a scrolling panel with `align-items: center` overlay works.

But does sticky footer hide the last shop item? It overlays while scrolling but at the end you can scroll such that last item is above footer because footer is in flow at end; sticky bottom means it's still occupying its space at the end, so no content permanently hidden. Good.

At desktop, no change.

Also move `.garage-foot p` hint maybe two lines; fine.

Let's also make shop descriptions less dim to improve readability on mobile (currently color var(--dim) #53616c, low contrast). On desktop too they're dim but legible. Could set `.shop-item .shop-info span { color: var(--muted); }` to improve overall readability. And stage info spans likewise. But the aesthetic is intentionally dim. The result "brief" text is very faint. For accessibility, bump to `#6d7f8c`. Let me adjust `--dim`? That affects everything. Better targeted to `var(--muted)` for these descriptions. `--muted` is #8496a4, more readable. Let's do shop and stage description to muted with maybe 0.9 opacity. On screenshot, descriptions "Cold-start the mule and settle it into a stable idle." are readable but faint. I'd improve.

